
<% content_for :title, "The Bare Minimum Day | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="minimum-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="minimum-hero">

    <picture class="minimum-background" aria-hidden="true">
      <source
        media="(min-width: 768px)"
        srcset="<%= asset_path('toolbox_bare_minimum_day_desktop.webp') %>"
      >
      <%= image_tag "toolbox_bare_minimum_day_mobile.webp",
          alt: "",
          loading: "eager" %>
    </picture>

    <div class="minimum-hero-content">

      <p class="minimum-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / TOOLBOX / THE BARE MINIMUM DAY
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>BARE MINIMUM.</span>
        <span>FULL CREDIT.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="minimum-intro">
        SOME DAYS, GETTING THROUGH THE DAY
        IS ENOUGH. WORK WITH WHAT YOU HAVE.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / TODAY'S CAPACITY -->
  <section class="minimum-section minimum-pink">
    <div class="minimum-inner">

      <p class="minimum-number">01 / TODAY'S CAPACITY</p>

      <h2>
        WHAT HAVE<br>
        YOU GOT<br>
        TODAY?
      </h2>

      <div class="minimum-copy">

        <p>
          Some days you wake up and know
          you're not functioning at your
          usual capacity. Maybe you're
          depressed, exhausted, overwhelmed,
          or dealing with another day
          of chronic pain. Things that
          ordinarily require little thought
          suddenly feel difficult.
        </p>

        <p>
          Your instinct might be to push
          through it. You look at everything
          you planned to do, everything
          you've fallen behind on, and
          everything you think you should
          be accomplishing.
        </p>

        <p>
          But today's capacity isn't
          determined by yesterday's
          productivity or tomorrow's
          responsibilities. It's determined
          by what your mind and body
          can realistically manage
          right now.
        </p>

        <p>
          There's a difference between
          being unwilling to do something
          and having genuinely limited
          capacity. Depression can make
          basic tasks exhausting.
          Chronic pain can make physical
          activity difficult.
          Poor sleep and emotional overload
          can make concentration and
          decision-making harder.
        </p>

        <p>
          Recognize what you're dealing
          with before deciding what
          you're going to demand
          of yourself.
        </p>

        <p>
          This isn't permission to ignore
          your health or responsibilities.
          It's an opportunity to separate
          what needs to happen from
          what you'd like to accomplish.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>
            You don't have to function
            at full capacity every day.
            You do need a realistic plan
            for the capacity you have.
          </strong>
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="minimum-callout">
        <p>
          CHECK IN: HOW IS YOUR MOOD?
          YOUR PAIN? YOUR ENERGY?
          YOUR SLEEP? WHAT CAN YOU
          REALISTICALLY MANAGE TODAY?
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / THE BASICS -->
  <section class="minimum-section minimum-black">
    <div class="minimum-inner">

      <p class="minimum-number">02 / THE BASICS</p>

      <h2>
        KISS IT:<br>
        KEEP IT<br>
        SIMPLE, STUPID.
      </h2>

      <div class="minimum-copy">

        <p>
          On a bare minimum day,
          your priorities become
          considerably smaller.
          You aren't trying to catch
          up on everything or make
          the day productive.
        </p>

        <p>
          Start with the things
          your body, health, and
          daily responsibilities
          actually require.
        </p>

        <p>
          Eat something. Drink water.
          Take your meds.
          Attend to basic hygiene. 
          Make sure Banjo has
          what he needs.
        </p>

        <p>
          These aren't impressive
          accomplishments, and they
          don't need to be. They're
          the things that keep your
          day manageable when you
          have limited resources.
        </p>

        <p>
          If a task is difficult,
          simplify how you do it
          rather than automatically
          abandoning it.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="minimum-grid">

        <div class="minimum-box">
          <h3>SOMETHING TO EAT.</h3>

          <p>
            You don't need to
            prepare an elaborate meal.
            A sandwich, leftovers,
            soup, cereal, or
            something easy to assemble
            is perfectly reasonable.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose food that requires
            minimal preparation,
            particularly when pain
            makes standing difficult.
          </p>

          <p>
            The goal is to eat
            enough to take care
            of yourself.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="minimum-box">
          <h3>SOMETHING TO DRINK.</h3>

          <p>
            Keep water nearby
            and drink throughout
            the day. If getting
            up frequently is
            difficult, prepare
            what you need
            ahead of time.
          </p>


          <p>
            Alcohol isn't part
            of your bare minimum.
            Sobriety remains
            a priority even
            on difficult days.
          </p>
        </div>


        <div class="minimum-box">
          <h3>BASIC COMFORT.</h3>

          <p>
            Wash your face,
            brush your teeth,
            change clothes,
            or shower if
            you're able.
          </p>

          <p>
                        Find a tolerable
            resting position
            and avoid movements
            that are painful.
          </p>

          <p>
            Make sure Banjo has
            food and water. Ask for help
            with his care if
            your mobility is limited.
          </p>

          <p>
            Comfort isn't a luxury
            you have to earn
            after being productive.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / MAKE IT SMALLER -->
  <section class="minimum-section minimum-yellow">
    <div class="minimum-inner">

      <p class="minimum-number">03 / MAKE IT SMALLER</p>

      <h2>
        LOWER THE<br>
        FUCKING BAR.
      </h2>

      <div class="minimum-copy">

        <p>
          Sometimes the problem
          isn't the task itself.
          It's the size of the
          task you've decided
          you need to complete.
        </p>

        <p>
          You think about cleaning
          the apartment, but the
          entire job sounds exhausting.
          You want to work on a
          creative project, but
          setting everything up
          feels like too much.
          You need to respond
          to messages, but
          the thought of several
          conversations is overwhelming.
        </p>

        <p>
          Instead of forcing
          the full version,
          look for a smaller one.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can wash one dish
          without cleaning the kitchen.
          You can respond to one
          important message without
          catching up on every
          conversation. You can
          spend five minutes
          making a stencil without
          using the Dremel.
        </p>

        <p>
          And sometimes even
          the smaller version
          is too much. If it
          isn't essential,
          you can leave it.
        </p>

        <p>
          The purpose isn't
          to squeeze productivity
          out of every available
          minute. It's to make
          necessary tasks possible
          without exhausting
          yourself further.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="minimum-grid">

        <div class="minimum-box">
          <h3>THE WHOLE TASK.</h3>

          <p>
            Clean the entire apartment.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cook a full meal.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finish a glass-engraving
            project.
          </p>

          <p>
            Catch up on every
            unanswered message.
          </p>

          <p>
            Take a shower
            and complete your
            usual routine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Complete everything
            on your to-do list.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="minimum-box">
          <h3>THE SMALLER VERSION.</h3>

          <p>
            Clear one surface
            or put away a few items.
          </p>

          <p>
            Make something simple
            or eat leftovers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Make a stencil
            for another day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Answer one important
            message.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wash your face,
            brush your teeth,
            or change clothes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose one necessary
            task and leave
            the rest.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <div class="minimum-callout">
        <p>
          IF THE TASK IS TOO MUCH,
          WHAT'S THE SMALLEST USEFUL
          VERSION? AND DOES IT
          ACTUALLY NEED TO
          HAPPEN TODAY?
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / WHAT CAN WAIT -->
  <section class="minimum-section minimum-purple">
    <div class="minimum-inner">

      <p class="minimum-number">04 / WHAT CAN WAIT</p>

      <h2>
        TODAY DOESN'T<br>
        NEED EVERYTHING.
      </h2>

      <div class="minimum-copy">

        <p>
          You have responsibilities,
          unfinished projects,
          appointments, plans,
          and things you'd like
          to accomplish.
          They don't all become
          urgent because you're
          having a difficult day.
        </p>

        <p>
          When your capacity is
          limited, prioritize
          according to actual
          consequences rather
          than guilt.
        </p>

        <p>
          Some things can't
          reasonably wait.
          Medication, 
          Banjo's
          needs, and genuinely
          time-sensitive obligations
          still require attention.
        </p>

        <p>
          But cleaning, organizing,
          hobbies, nonessential
          errands, and most personal
          projects can usually
          be postponed.
        </p>

        <p>
          You might feel frustrated
          that you're not doing
          more. Especially when
          pain or depression has
          already taken away
          activities you enjoy.
        </p>

        <p>
          That frustration doesn't
          change today's capacity.
          You can acknowledge
          what you're missing
          without forcing yourself
          to do something
          you're not up for.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>
            Something can matter
            without it needing to
            happen today.
          </strong>
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="minimum-grid">

        <div class="minimum-box">
          <h3>WHAT NEEDS ATTENTION?</h3>

          <p>
            Prescribed medication
            and essential medical care.
          </p>

          <p>
            Food, hydration,
            basic hygiene,
            and necessary rest.
          </p>

          <p>
            Banjo's care.
          </p>

          <p>
            Important appointments,
            deadlines, and obligations
            with real consequences.
          </p>

          <p>
            Significant changes
            in mood
          </p>

          <p>
            If you can't handle
            something essential,
            arrange assistance.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="minimum-box">
          <h3>WHAT CAN WAIT?</h3>

          <p>
            Deep cleaning
            and organizing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Optional errands
            and shopping.
          </p>

          <p>
            Website updates,
            creative projects,
            and hobbies.
          </p>

          <p>
            Nonurgent messages
            and social obligations.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tasks that don't
            have an immediate
            consequence if
            they're postponed.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can return
            to these when
            you have more
            capacity.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 / CONNECTION -->
  <section class="minimum-section minimum-black">
    <div class="minimum-inner">

      <p class="minimum-number">05 / CONNECTION</p>

      <h2>
        LET SOMEONE<br>
        KNOW.
      </h2>

      <div class="minimum-copy">

        <p>
          When you're having
          a difficult day,
          withdrawing can
          feel easier than
          explaining yourself.
          You may not want
          to talk, answer
          questions, or
          deal with anyone.
        </p>

        <p>
          You don't have to
          socialize or pretend
          you're feeling better
          than you are.
          But there's a
          difference between
          choosing a quiet day
          and disappearing
          when you need support.
        </p>

        <p>
          Sometimes a short
          message is enough.
          Let someone know
          you're having a
          low-capacity day.
          You can tell them
          whether you need
          company, practical
          assistance, or
          simply some space.
        </p>

       

        <p>
          If drinking starts
          sounding appealing
          because you're
          uncomfortable,
          bored, or miserable,
          tell someone in
          your recovery circle.
          Follow your established
          relapse plan.
        </p>

        <p>
          A bare minimum day
          isn't a reason to
          ignore important
          warning signs.
          Getting appropriate
          support can be
          one of today's
          essential tasks.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="minimum-callout">
        <p>
          "I'M HAVING A PRETTY ROUGH
          DAY AND DON'T HAVE MUCH
          ENERGY. I'M KEEPING THINGS
          SIMPLE TODAY. I DON'T
          REALLY FEEL LIKE TALKING
          MUCH, BUT I WANTED
          TO CHECK IN."
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 / YOUR MINIMUM -->
  <section class="minimum-section minimum-pink">
    <div class="minimum-inner">

      <p class="minimum-number">06 / YOUR MINIMUM</p>

      <h2>
        YOUR DAY.<br>
        YOUR CAPACITY.
      </h2>

      <div class="minimum-copy">

        <p>
          Your bare minimum won't
          look exactly the same
          every day. What you
          can manage during a
          depressive episode may
          be different from what
          you can manage during
          a severe pain flare.
        </p>

        <p>
          Some days you'll have
          enough energy to complete
          a few tasks, enjoy
          something creative,
          and spend time with
          other people.
        </p>

        <p>
          Other days, eating,
          taking your medications,
          caring for Banjo,
          and getting through
          the afternoon may
          require most of
          what you've got.
        </p>

        <p>
          You don't need to
          compare those days.
          A useful plan accounts
          for the circumstances
          you're actually in.
        </p>

        <p>
          The bare minimum
          isn't a competition
          to see how little
          you can do. It's
          a way to identify
          what's essential
          when doing everything
          isn't realistic.
        </p>

        <p>
          Keep your responsibilities
          manageable. Protect your
          health and sobriety.
          Ask for help when
          you need it. Allow
          nonessential things
          to wait.
        </p>

        <p>
          And if you feel better
          later, you can always
          decide to do more.
          You don't owe the
          day anything beyond
          what is necessary
          and reasonable.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="minimum-grid">

        <div class="minimum-box">
          <h3>MY ESSENTIALS.</h3>

          <p>
            Take meds.
          </p>

          <p>
            Eat something
            and stay hydrated.
          </p>

          <p>
            Meet Banjo's
            basic needs.
          </p>

      

          <p>
            Maintain basic hygiene.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stay sober.
          </p>

          <p>
            Address anything
            genuinely urgent
            or time-sensitive.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="minimum-box">
          <h3>MY SUPPORT PLAN.</h3>

          <p>
            Tell someone if
            the day is becoming
            difficult to manage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ask for practical
            help with tasks
            you can't physically
            complete yourself.
          </p>

          <p>
            Contact your psych
            or therapist if
            mood symptoms are
            worsening or
            safety concerns arise.
          </p>

          <p>
            Contact your doc about significant
            changes in pain.
          </p>

          <p>
            Reach out to recovery
            circle if you're
            considering drinking.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use your existing
            safety and relapse
            plans when needed.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <div class="minimum-callout">
        <p>
          YOU DON'T HAVE TO MAKE
          TODAY PRODUCTIVE TO
          MAKE IT WORTHWHILE.
          TAKE CARE OF WHAT
          MATTERS. LET THE
          REST WAIT.
        </p>
      </div>
